<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Bishopric of Dorpat</h1><p class="st-meta">1224 CE – 1558 CE · capital Dorpat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Prince-bishopric of south-eastern Estonia centred on Dorpat (Tartu), a prosperous Hanseatic frontier facing Pskov, until Ivan the Terrible took it in 1558.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-of-buxhoeveden">Hermann of Buxhoeveden</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Bishop Albert of Riga</a>, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> in 1220 and moved his see to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a> after its capture in 1224. The bishopric took over the former lands of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ugandi">Ugandi</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vaiga">Vaiga</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mohu">Mõhu</a>, and Hermann received them as a prince of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> ruled through a cathedral chapter and German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, sharing power with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a> and the diets of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/terra-mariana">Terra Mariana</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a>, a Hanseatic member, grew rich from trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> and Novgorod and built a large Gothic cathedral. Estonian peasants worked the land under increasing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>At the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV of Russia</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a> in 1558, and the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-wesel">Hermann Wesel</a>, was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, where he died. The territory was later contested by Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/swedish-livonia">Swedish Livonia</a> in the 1620s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bishopric-of-dorpat.jpg" alt="Bishopric of Dorpat" width="320" height="347"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Termer assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medieval_Livonia_1260.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1224 CE – 1558 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dorpat</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hermann of Buxhoeveden, Hermann Wesel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Low German, Latin, Estonian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/estonia">Estonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Prince-Bishopric of Dorpat, Stift Dorpat, Tartu bishopric</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1391&amp;focus=bishopric-of-dorpat" aria-label="Bishopric of Dorpat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5248 208 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bishopric of Dorpat lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5568 464L5539 500M5568 464L5229 694M5568 464L5820 533M5568 464L5572 470M5568 464L5572 457M5568 464L5551 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5539 500h0M5229 694h0M5820 533h0M5572 470h0M5572 457h0M5551 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5568 464h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5568 464h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1391&amp;focus=bishopric-of-dorpat">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
